Consider $f(x, y) = x^2 + y^4$, which has a (global) minimum at $(0, 0)$ since $f(x, y) >= 0$ for all $(x, y)$. Here
$
  bold(nabla) f = (2x, 4y^3), quad matbold(H) = mat(2, 0; 0, 12y^2),
$
and at the stationary point $(0, 0)$ the Hessian matrix is
$ matbold(H) = mat(2, 0; 0, 0). $
Its eigenvalues are $lambda_1 = 2 > 0$ and $lambda_2 = 0$, so the Hessian is positive semi-definite. The quadratic terms do not classify the stationary point, and higher order terms of the Taylor series are needed.
